  "conclusions": [
    "Diverse real samples reverse the tiny-prompt result: the missing down-output residual has a learnable linear component across a disjoint corpus partition.",
    "The linear-kernel dual folds into the compact retained down matrix and matches dual predictions below 7.5e-7 relative L2, so no support bank is required at inference.",
    "Live replacement of the one folded expert passes the small held-out NLL regression gate: relative perplexity changes by -1.47% over 132 tokens, while a full-width injection control changes it by only +0.082% on the paired subset.",
    "The apparent NLL improvement is not a quality-improvement claim: the sample is small, single-domain, and covers only one of 6,144 experts.",
    "Routing-preselected early, middle, and late experts also remain non-regressing when injected separately, but optimal ridge varies from 1.0 to 3.0 and local improvement varies from 2.8% to 6.1%.",
    "Independent single-expert results do not establish safe composition; simultaneous multi-expert injection is the next quality gate.",
    "Promotion remains false. Scale the same sealed-corpus control across representative layers and experts before considering artifact or Metal-kernel work; joint gate/up/down training remains necessary for a hardware-tier capacity reduction."
  ]
